WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.800
Here we go!

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:10.000
Since the 1960s, the percent
of the US population that moves in any given year

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.800
has fallen by 50%.
Research suggests that Americans today

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:18.960
are less geographically mobile than at
any point since the late 1940s. There's a

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.100
bigger problem here than just boring
scenery: A mobile, opportunistic workforce

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.939
can claim higher salaries and make our
economy more dynamic. So what happened?

00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:30.840
Several things! Millenials are moving out
and starting their own lives at an

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.160
historically low pace. Older people feel
trapped in undervalued homes, waiting on

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.300
the housing market. And some safety net
programs trap vulnerable Americans in place,

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.870
instead of helping them move to new
opportunities. What can we do about it?

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:46.890
To start with, we could redirect some
safety net funding towards relocation

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:50.760
vouchers, and make social programs more
flexible. Let's empower people to move

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.260
instead of anchoring them in place. But
policy can only do so much. The core

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:59.489
issue is culture! We need to rediscover
the entrepreneurial frontier spirit that

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:03.480
defined our country for generations. For
America to really get moving again,

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.140
Americans will need to get moving again.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.340
To learn more about how to get Americans

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:13.080
moving again, check the links in the
description below. Also, let us know what

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:18.170
other topics you want a AEI scholars to
cover in 60 seconds.

